      "traceTextDiagram": "Skill Execution Framework\n├── Main() generic entry point \u003c-- 1a\n│   ├── mainWithCode() orchestrates execution \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:98\n│   │   ├── Load configuration \u0026 context \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:118\n│   │   ├── Parse JSON input from stdin \u003c-- 1b\n│   │   ├── Validate input struct fields \u003c-- 1c\n│   │   ├── Execute skill function \u003c-- 1d\n│   │   └── Handle errors \u0026 emit envelopes \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:168\n│   └── Bootstrap() for custom entrypoints \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:87\n└── RunContext provides dependencies \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/context.go:21\n    ├── CAS storage \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/context.go:26\n    ├── Path validator \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/context.go:41\n    └── Output formatter \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/context.go:47",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nFoxctl needs a **standardized way to execute file operations** across different skills while maintaining security and consistency. Without this framework, each skill would need to implement its own input parsing, validation, error handling, and output formatting - leading to inconsistent behavior and potential security vulnerabilities. The skill execution framework solves this by providing a **unified pipeline** that all filesystem skills (read, write, edit) can inherit, ensuring proper validation, structured output, and integration with the content-addressable storage system.\n\n## Details\n\nThe skill framework follows a **generic execution pattern** starting from the `Main()` function [1a] which handles all skills uniformly. It first loads configuration and establishes a `RunContext` containing dependencies like CAS storage and path validation. Input is parsed as JSON from stdin [1b] and validated using struct tags [1c] to catch errors early. The actual skill logic is then executed [1d] with all necessary dependencies injected. If anything fails, the framework automatically formats and emits structured error envelopes. This design allows skills to focus purely on their core logic while the framework handles cross-cutting concerns like security validation, CAS integration, and consistent output formatting."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "title": "File Reading with CAS Integration",
      "description": "Secure file reading pipeline with symlink resolution and content-addressable storage",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "2a",
          "path": "skills/fs_read/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 35,
          "lineContent": "validPath, err := skillmain.ValidatePath(rc, in.Path)",
          "title": "Path Validation",
          "description": "Security validation of file path"
        },
        {
          "id": "2b",
          "path": "skills/fs_read/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 41,
          "lineContent": "resolved, err := filepath.EvalSymlinks(validPath)",
          "title": "Symlink Resolution",
          "description": "Prevents TOCTOU race conditions"
        },
        {
          "id": "2c",
          "path": "skills/fs_read/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 53,
          "lineContent": "file, err := os.Open(resolved)",
          "title": "File Opening",
          "description": "Secure file handle creation"
        },
        {
          "id": "2d",
          "path": "skills/fs_read/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 70,
          "lineContent": "obj, err := rc.CASStore.Put(ctx, file, kind, []string{\"fs_read\"})",
          "title": "CAS Storage",
          "description": "Stores file content in content-addressable storage"
        },
        {
          "id": "2e",
          "path": "skills/fs_read/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 113,
          "lineContent": "return skillout.Emit(rc, \"fs/read\", data)",
          "title": "Result Output",
          "description": "Emits structured result with CAS hints"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "File Reading with CAS Integration\n├── skillmain.Main() entry point \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:81\n│   └── fs_read.run() function \u003c-- skills/fs_read/main.go:34\n│       ├── ValidatePath() security check \u003c-- 2a\n│       ├── filepath.EvalSymlinks() resolution \u003c-- 2b\n│       ├── os.Open() file handle creation \u003c-- 2c\n│       ├── CASStore.Put() storage operation \u003c-- 2d\n│       └── skillout.Emit() result output \u003c-- 2e\n└── CAS Storage Backend \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:62\n    ├── sha256.New() hash creation \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:85\n    ├── content streaming \u0026 hashing \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:97\n    └── file storage with metadata \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:162",
      "traceGuide": "# Motivation\n\nThe filesystem read skill solves the **critical security problem** of safely reading files within an AI coding environment. Without proper controls, AI agents could accidentally read sensitive files outside the intended workspace, follow malicious symlinks, or overwhelm systems with large file reads. This skill provides **secure, sandboxed file access** with automatic content-addressable storage integration for efficient handling of large files and backup capabilities.\n\n# Details\n\nThe read operation follows a **strict security pipeline** starting with path validation [2a] that ensures the requested file stays within allowed directories. The system then resolves symlinks [2b] to prevent TOCTOU (time-of-check-to-time-of-use) race conditions where an attacker could change a symlink after validation but before file access. Once security checks pass, the file is opened [2c] and its content is automatically stored in the CAS system [2d] for deduplication and future access. Finally, the skill emits structured output [2e] containing file metadata, a preview of the content, and CAS retrieval hints for large files. The CAS integration ensures that reading the same large file multiple times doesn't consume additional storage, while providing built-in backup and versioning capabilities."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "title": "File Writing with Multiple Modes",
      "description": "Controlled file writing with create/overwrite/append modes and CAS content retrieval",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "3a",
          "path": "skills/fs_write/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 54,
          "lineContent": "targetPath, err := skillmain.ValidatePath(rc, in.Path, skillmain.WithPathMessage(\"resolve target path\"))",
          "title": "Target Path Validation",
          "description": "Validates destination path"
        },
        {
          "id": "3b",
          "path": "skills/fs_write/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 60,
          "lineContent": "content, err := getContent(ctx, rc, in)",
          "title": "Content Retrieval",
          "description": "Gets content from input or CAS"
        },
        {
          "id": "3c",
          "path": "skills/fs_write/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 72,
          "lineContent": "if err := checkWriteMode(targetPath, in.Mode); err != nil {",
          "title": "Write Mode Check",
          "description": "Validates create/overwrite/append mode"
        },
        {
          "id": "3d",
          "path": "skills/fs_write/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 85,
          "lineContent": "bytesWritten, checksum, err := performWrite(targetPath, content, in.Mode, perm)",
          "title": "File Writing",
          "description": "Performs actual file write operation"
        },
        {
          "id": "3e",
          "path": "skills/fs_write/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 169,
          "lineContent": "f, err = os.OpenFile(path, os.O_CREATE|os.O_WRONLY|os.O_TRUNC, perm)",
          "title": "File Handle Creation",
          "description": "Creates file with appropriate flags"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "File Writing Operation Flow\n├── skillmain.Main() entry point \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:81\n│   └── run() function execution \u003c-- skills/fs_write/main.go:36\n│       ├── Path validation \u003c-- 3a\n│       ├── Content retrieval \u003c-- 3b\n│       ├── Write mode check \u003c-- 3c\n│       └── performWrite() execution \u003c-- 3d\n│           ├── parsePermissions() \u003c-- skills/fs_write/main.go:66\n│           ├── checkWriteMode()\n│           └── File handle creation \u003c-- 3e\n│               ├── os.OpenFile() with flags\n│               ├── File write operation \u003c-- skills/fs_write/main.go:185\n│               └── Checksum calculation \u003c-- skills/fs_write/main.go:191\n└── skillout.Emit() result output \u003c-- skills/fs_write/main.go:105",
      "traceGuide": "# Motivation\n\nThe file writing system solves the problem of **secure, controlled file manipulation** in an AI agent environment. When AI agents need to write files, they must operate within strict security boundaries to prevent accidental or malicious file system damage. This system provides **three distinct write modes** (create, overwrite, append) with built-in validation, permission control, and content verification through checksums. It ensures agents can only write to approved locations while maintaining auditability and preventing unintended data loss.\n\n# Details\n\nThe writing process begins with **path validation** [3a] using the security framework to ensure the target location is within allowed boundaries. The system then retrieves content either directly from input or from the **content-addressable storage** [3b], enabling efficient handling of large files. Before writing, it performs a **write mode check** [3c] to enforce the create/overwrite/append policy, preventing accidental overwrites when create mode is specified. The actual file operation [3d] involves creating a file handle with appropriate OS flags [3e], writing the content, and generating a **SHA256 checksum** for integrity verification. This comprehensive approach ensures every write operation is traceable, secure, and verifiable."
    },
    {
