      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "File Edit Application Pipeline\n├── fs_apply_edit skill entry point \u003c-- skills/fs_apply_edit/main.go:71\n│   ├── Path validation for target file \u003c-- 4a\n│   ├── editutil.ApplyFile() call \u003c-- skills/fs_apply_edit/main.go:120\n│   │   ├── Read original file content \u003c-- 4c\n│   │   ├── Apply edit transformations \u003c-- 4d\n│   │   └── Create backup in CAS storage \u003c-- 4e\n│   └── Return edit results with metadata \u003c-- skills/fs_apply_edit/main.go:148\n└── editutil.ApplyFile() implementation \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/editutil/file.go:34\n    ├── File reading \u0026 validation \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/editutil/file.go:39\n    ├── Edit transformation function \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/editutil/file.go:45\n    │   └── Apply individual edits (fuzzy/regex) \u003c-- skills/fs_apply_edit/main.go:152\n    ├── Backup creation (if enabled) \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/editutil/file.go:60\n    └── File writing with diff generation \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/editutil/file.go:80",
      "traceGuide": "# Motivation\n\nWhen editing files programmatically, you need **safe, reversible operations** that won't corrupt data. The fs/apply_edit system solves this by providing sophisticated text editing with automatic backups, fuzzy matching for robustness, and comprehensive validation to prevent destructive changes.\n\n# Details\n\nThe system starts by **validating the target path** [4a] to ensure it's within allowed boundaries. It then reads the original file content [4c] and applies a series of edit transformations [4d] using different matching strategies:\n\n- **Exact matching** for precise replacements\n- **Fuzzy matching** with whitespace tolerance for robustness  \n- **Regex matching** for complex patterns\n\nBefore writing changes, the system **creates a backup** in content-addressable storage [4e], storing the original content with a unique digest. This enables perfect rollback capabilities. The edit application happens through a functional transformation pipeline where the original content flows through all edits sequentially, producing the final modified text.\n\nThe system generates diffs and provides detailed feedback about which lines were changed, making it transparent about what operations were performed. This approach ensures **atomic, traceable, and reversible** file modifications suitable for automated workflows."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "title": "Security and Path Validation",
      "description": "Comprehensive path security system preventing directory traversal and symlink attacks",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "5a",
          "path": "internal/domain/policy/pathvalidator.go",
          "lineNumber": 84,
          "lineContent": "func (v *PathValidator) ValidatePath(userPath string) (string, error) {",
          "title": "Path Validation Entry",
          "description": "Main path validation function"
        },
        {
          "id": "5b",
          "path": "internal/domain/policy/pathvalidator.go",
          "lineNumber": 105,
          "lineContent": "canonical, usedSymlink, err := v.resolve(absPath)",
          "title": "Path Resolution",
          "description": "Resolves path with symlink tracking"
        },
        {
          "id": "5c",
          "path": "internal/domain/policy/pathvalidator.go",
          "lineNumber": 114,
          "lineContent": "if v.hasPrefix(canonical, v.workspace) {",
          "title": "Workspace Check",
          "description": "Ensures path is within workspace"
        },
        {
          "id": "5d",
          "path": "internal/domain/policy/pathvalidator.go",
          "lineNumber": 118,
          "lineContent": "if v.pathInsideRoots(canonical) {",
          "title": "Allowed Roots Check",
          "description": "Checks against additional allowed directories"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Path Security System\n├── skillmain.ValidatePath() \u003c-- 5a\n│   └── PathValidator.ValidatePath() \u003c-- 5a\n│       ├── Input validation\n│       │   └── UTF-8 \u0026 null byte checks \u003c-- internal/domain/policy/pathvalidator.go:89\n│       ├── Path resolution\n│       │   └── v.resolve(absPath) \u003c-- 5b\n│       │       ├── resolveWithSymlinks() \u003c-- internal/domain/policy/pathvalidator.go:158\n│       │       └── ensureNoSymlink() \u003c-- internal/domain/policy/pathvalidator.go:198\n│       └── Boundary enforcement\n│           ├── Workspace check \u003c-- 5c\n│           └── Allowed roots check \u003c-- 5d\n└── Security boundaries\n    ├── Workspace directory \u003c-- internal/domain/policy/pathvalidator.go:76\n    └── Additional allowed roots \u003c-- internal/domain/policy/pathvalidator.go:77",
      "traceGuide": "# Motivation\n\nWhen AI agents manipulate files, they need **security barriers** to prevent malicious or accidental access outside intended directories. Without proper validation, an agent could be tricked into reading `/etc/passwd`, overwriting system files, or following malicious symlinks to sensitive locations. The path security system solves this by creating a **sandboxed environment** where file operations are strictly bounded to approved directories.\n\n# Details\n\nThe security system starts with `skillmain.ValidatePath()` [5a], which delegates to the `PathValidator.ValidatePath()` method. This performs **multiple layers of validation**:\n\nFirst, it checks for **invalid input** like null bytes or non-UTF8 characters that could bypass security checks [5a]. Then it resolves the path, handling symlinks safely to prevent **TOCTOU race conditions** [5b]. The system tracks whether symlinks were used and can either follow them safely or reject them entirely.\n\nFinally, it enforces **boundary constraints** by ensuring the resolved path stays within the workspace directory [5c] or additional allowed roots [5d]. This creates a secure perimeter where agents can operate freely without risking system-wide damage. The validator is configured during skill initialization with the workspace path and any additional safe directories like temp folders or user home directories."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "title": "Content-Addressable Storage System",
      "description": "CAS implementation for file storage with deduplication and verification",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "6a",
          "path": "internal/storage/cas/file_store.go",
          "lineNumber": 81,
          "lineContent": "func (s *Store) Put(ctx context.Context, r io.Reader, kind string, tags []string) (Object, error) {",
          "title": "CAS Storage Entry",
          "description": "Stores content with SHA256 hashing"
        },
        {
          "id": "6b",
          "path": "internal/storage/cas/file_store.go",
          "lineNumber": 85,
          "lineContent": "h := sha256.New()",
          "title": "Hash Initialization",
          "description": "Creates SHA256 hash for content"
        },
        {
          "id": "6c",
          "path": "internal/storage/cas/file_store.go",
          "lineNumber": 118,
          "lineContent": "digest := \"sha256:\" + hex.EncodeToString(h.Sum(nil))",
          "title": "Digest Generation",
          "description": "Generates content digest"
        },
        {
          "id": "6d",
          "path": "internal/storage/cas/file_store.go",
          "lineNumber": 121,
          "lineContent": "if obj, handled, err := s.handleDuplicateObject(digest, tmp, tags); err != nil {",
          "title": "Deduplication Check",
          "description": "Handles duplicate content efficiently"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "CAS Storage System\n├── Store.Put() entry point \u003c-- 6a\n│   ├── Initialize SHA256 hash \u003c-- 6b\n│   │   ├── Stream content to temp file \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:86\n│   │   │   └── Generate content digest \u003c-- 6c\n│   │   └── Handle duplicate check \u003c-- 6d\n│   │       ├── Check if digest exists \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:135\n│   │       └── Merge tags if duplicate \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:148\n│   └── Finalize upload \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:162\n│       ├── Move temp to final location \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:173\n│       └── Write metadata JSON \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:183\n└── Supporting operations\n    ├── pathForDigest() validation \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:414\n    └── writeMetadata() persistence \u003c-- internal/storage/cas/file_store.go:383",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nThe Content-Addressable Storage (CAS) system solves the **critical problem of efficient file storage and deduplication** in foxctl. When skills read, write, or edit files, the system needs to store large content safely, avoid duplicates, and provide reliable backup mechanisms. CAS ensures that identical files are stored only once, enables content verification through SHA256 hashes, and supports features like automatic backups during file edits.\n\n## Details\n\nThe CAS system operates as a **hash-based storage layer** where files are stored by their content fingerprint rather than filename. When content is stored [6a], the system creates a SHA256 hash [6b] and uses it as the storage key. This hash becomes the content's unique identifier [6c].\n\nThe system implements **intelligent deduplication** - before storing new content, it checks if that hash already exists [6d]. If found, it merges tags instead of creating duplicates. Content is streamed to temporary files first, then atomically moved to their final location using the hash as the filename.\n\nMetadata is stored alongside each object in JSON format, tracking creation time, content type, and searchable tags. The system also supports **pinning** important objects to prevent garbage collection, and includes a **verifying reader** that ensures content integrity by re-hashing during retrieval.\n\nThis design enables foxctl's filesystem skills to provide features like automatic backups during edits, efficient large file handling, and content verification - all while maintaining security and performance."
    }
  ],
  "description": "The filesystem skills system provides secure file operations with CAS integration, featuring read/write/edit capabilities with comprehensive security controls and backup mechanisms. Key components include the skill execution framework [1a], secure file reading with CAS storage [2d], multi-mode file writing [3d], advanced editing with fuzzy matching [4b], path security validation [5a], and the underlying content-addressable storage system [6a].",
  "mermaidDiagram": "graph TB\n    subgraph Framework[\"Skill Execution Framework\"]\n        1a[\"1a: Skill Entry Point\u003cbr/\u003eMain function\"]\n        1b[\"1b: Input Parsing\u003cbr/\u003eJSON decode\"]\n        1c[\"1c: Input Validation\u003cbr/\u003eStruct validation\"]\n        1d[\"1d: Skill Execution\u003cbr/\u003eRun function\"]\n        1a --\u003e 1b --\u003e 1c --\u003e 1d\n    end\n\n    subgraph FileOps[\"File Operations\"]\n        subgraph Read[\"File Reading\"]\n            2a[\"2a: Path Validation\"]\n            2b[\"2b: Symlink Resolution\"]\n            2c[\"2c: File Opening\"]\n            2d[\"2d: CAS Storage\"]\n            2e[\"2e: Result Output\"]\n            2a --\u003e 2b --\u003e 2c --\u003e 2d --\u003e 2e\n        end\n\n        subgraph Write[\"File Writing\"]\n            3a[\"3a: Target Path Validation\"]\n            3b[\"3b: Content Retrieval\"]\n            3c[\"3c: Write Mode Check\"]\n            3d[\"3d: File Writing\"]\n            3e[\"3e: File Handle Creation\"]\n            3a --\u003e 3b --\u003e 3c --\u003e 3d\n            3d --\u003e 3e\n        end\n\n        subgraph Edit[\"File Editing\"]\n            4a[\"4a: Path Validation\"]\n            4b[\"4b: Edit Application\"]\n            4c[\"4c: File Reading\"]\n            4d[\"4d: Edit Transformation\"]\n            4e[\"4e: Backup Creation\"]\n            4a --\u003e 4b --\u003e 4c --\u003e 4d --\u003e 4e\n        end\n    end\n\n    subgraph Security[\"Security \u0026 Storage\"]\n        subgraph PathVal[\"Path Validation\"]\n            5a[\"5a: Validation Entry\"]\n            5b[\"5b: Path Resolution\"]\n            5c[\"5c: Workspace Check\"]\n            5d[\"5d: Allowed Roots Check\"]\n            5a --\u003e 5b --\u003e 5c --\u003e 5d\n        end\n\n        subgraph CAS[\"Content-Addressable Storage\"]\n            6a[\"6a: CAS Storage Entry\"]\n            6b[\"6b: Hash Initialization\"]\n            6c[\"6c: Digest Generation\"]\n            6d[\"6d: Deduplication Check\"]\n            6a --\u003e 6b --\u003e 6c --\u003e 6d\n        end\n    end\n\n    1d --\u003e|executes| 2a\n    1d --\u003e|executes| 3a\n    1d --\u003e|executes| 4a\n\n    2a --\u003e|validates path| 5a\n    2d --\u003e|stores content| 6a\n\n    3a --\u003e|validates path| 5a\n    3b --\u003e|retrieves from| 6a\n\n    4a --\u003e|validates path| 5a\n    4e --\u003e|backs up to| 6a\n\n    style Framework fill:#a5d8ff\n    style Read fill:#fcc2d7\n    style Write fill:#d0bfff\n    style Edit fill:#b2f2bb\n    style PathVal fill:#ffec99\n    style CAS fill:#ffd8a8"
